K.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, spacing: { after: 120 }, children: [new TextRun({ text: 'Final step-wise methodology', font: FONT, size: 30, color: '2C5F2D' })] }));
K.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, spacing: { after: 500 }, children: [new TextRun({ text: 'Ph.D. (Agricultural Physics), ICAR-IARI  |  UAV RGB, multispectral, hyperspectral and thermal data  |  4-class severity classification and disease-index regression', font: FONT, size: 20, italics: true, color: '555555' })] }));
K.push(NOTE(`**How each step is written.** Every step has the same parts: **Position** (which step it comes after and which step uses its output), **Concept**, **Why it is needed at this position**, **Importance and justification** (why this method, with references), **How it is done**, **How it is used with my data**, a **figure or table with dummy data**, the **Output**, **Notes** on other methods with references, **Interview questions**, and **References** for the step. Dummy data only illustrate what the results will look like; they are not results.`));
K.push(KEY('The methodology in two minutes:', `White rust changes pigments, leaf structure, water status, transpiration and canopy texture; each UAV sensor sees part of this. **Common steps** build a pooled table of plot-dates from a 72-plot field experiment, with the disease index (DI) and four classes: **Healthy (DI = 0), Low (0 < DI ≤ 25%), Moderate (25 < DI ≤ 50%), High (DI > 50%)**. **Objective 1** finds, for each sensor, the features that respond to white rust: ANOVA and correlation with DI, a hyperspectral correlation scalogram with ReliefF, a White Rust Index, a stepwise discriminant analysis between the classes, and Boruta, giving disease-specific feature (DSF) pools for classification and for DI regression. **Objective 2** reduces each pool with VIF and ML-SFFS and compares six models on each sensor and on four sensor combinations (ML-SFFS fusion and two deep-learning fusions). **Objective 3** repeats the classification with data from before symptoms. Every selection and tuning step is done inside training replications (leave-one-replication-out), and the final models are tested at Bharatpur and in farmers' fields.`));
K.push(new TableOfContents('Contents', { hyperlink: true, headingStyleRange: '1-2' }));
K.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// ======================= 1 OBJECTIVES =======================
K.push(H1('1. Objectives, tasks and overall workflow'));
K.push(P(`The thesis has three objectives (ORW): (1) to characterise white rust of mustard through multimodal UAV sensing; (2) to detect and discriminate disease severity through AI-based multimodal data fusion; (3) to predict white rust at the pre-symptomatic stage using machine learning. They are addressed through three tasks (${T('tasks')}) and the workflow of ${F('master')}.`));
K.push(...tab2('tasks', [
  ['Task', 'Target', 'Samples', 'Objectives'],
  ['C. Severity classification', 'Four classes: Healthy (DI = 0), Low (0 < DI ≤ 25%), Moderate (25 < DI ≤ 50%), High (DI > 50%)', 'All post-symptomatic plot-dates', '1, 2'],
  ['R. Severity regression', 'Disease index DI (%), continuous', 'All post-symptomatic plot-dates', '1, 2'],
  ['P. Pre-symptomatic prediction', 'Healthy vs later-infected', 'Plot-dates before visible symptoms', '3'],
], [2300, 3400, 2300, 1306], 'The three tasks of the thesis. Including Healthy as a class means that detection (healthy vs diseased) is part of the 4-class task.', 16));
K.push(...fig2('master', 'm0_master.png', 'Master flow chart of the final methodology: common steps A1–A4, Objective 1 (Steps 1.1–1.6), Objective 2 (Steps 2.1–2.6) and Objective 3 (Steps 3.1–3.4).', 500));
K.push(H2('1.1 Why these methods: choice based on the reviewed papers'));
K.push(P(`${T('choice')} lists, for every decision, the method chosen, the alternatives used in the reviewed papers, and the reason for the choice in this situation (UAV plot data, four sensors, 72 plots observed repeatedly, overlapping severity classes).`));
K.push(...tab2('choice', [
  ['Decision', 'Chosen', 'Alternatives in the papers', 'Reason for the choice'],
  ['Severity target', '4 classes + DI regression', 'Binary only (Mahlein 2013); DI only (Liu 2024; Guo 2021)', 'Gives both a management class and a continuous estimate; DI regression avoids class-limit problems'],
  ['Pooling of dates', 'All dates pooled', 'Date- or stage-wise models (Guo 2021)', 'Only pooled data contain all severity levels; one model for any flight (Liu 2024; Feng 2022)'],
  ['Validation', 'Leave-one-replication-out, selection inside folds', 'Random split (Shukla 2024: 80/20); independent experiment (Feng 2022)', 'Repeated plot-dates are dependent (Roberts 2017); RBD replications are balanced test sets'],
  ['Univariate statistics', 'One-way ANOVA + Tukey HSD; Pearson r with DI', 't-test only (Zhang 2012a; Tian 2021); Dunnett test (Liu 2024)', 'Four classes need a multi-group test; ANOVA + Tukey is the standard, easily explained choice in agricultural research (Gomez and Gomez 1984)'],
  ['HS band selection', 'Correlation scalogram + ReliefF; one band per correlated region', 'Separability scalogram (Tian 2021); SPA (Zhu 2017); PCA/PLSR loadings (Shukla 2024)', 'Classes overlap: a DI-based scalogram needs no class limits (regression) and ReliefF is built for overlapping multi-class data (classification); used for mustard (Shukla 2024) and severity (Meng 2020)'],
  ['Disease index', 'White Rust Index (ND + wR)', 'General indices only; ROTI (Bhattacharya 2013); RBI (Mandal 2023)', 'Disease-specific index reduces confusion with other stresses (Mahlein 2013)'],
  ['Class discrimination', 'Two-step stepwise discriminant analysis (LDA)', 'FLDA (Zhang 2012a); CART + JM (Das 2020)', 'Mustard precedent (Bhattacharya 2013); simple, interpretable functions and a score plot of the classes'],
  ['Relevant features', 'Boruta per sensor and task', 'RFE (Liu 2024); correlation filter (Guo 2021)', 'All-relevant, non-linear, statistically tested (Kursa and Rudnicki 2010; Mustafa 2023)'],
  ['Optimum subset', 'VIF < 10 → ML-SFFS', 'SPA (Huang 2020); full feature set', 'Removes redundancy then finds compact subsets; 2–4 features were enough in Tian 2021'],
  ['Models', 'SVM/SVR, RF/RFR, k-NN, PLSR, DNN-F1, DNN-F2', 'BPNN, ELM, LS-SVM (Zhu 2017); MARS (Mandal 2023); 3D-CNN (Nguyen 2023)', 'Cover kernel, tree, instance, linear and deep families; all used in the core papers'],
  ['Fusion', 'ML-SFFS feature-level fusion; DNN-F1 and DNN-F2', 'End-to-end image fusion (Deng 2024); decision-level', 'Plot-level features suit 72 plots; DNN-F2 best in Maimaitijiang 2020; ML-SFFS fusion interpretable (Mustafa 2023)'],
  ['Pre-symptomatic labels', 'Infected only if symptoms appear later', 'Days after inoculation (Zhu 2017; Terentev 2023)', 'Field inoculation does not guarantee infection (Tian 2021)'],
], [1700, 2200, 2700, 2706], 'Method choices for this thesis and their justification from the reviewed papers.', 15));

K.push(H2('1.2 How the overlap between severity classes is handled'));
K.push(P(`**Why classes overlap.** The four classes are cuts of a continuous disease index at 0, 25 and 50%. A plot with DI = 24% (Low) and one with DI = 26% (Moderate) are almost identical for any sensor, so neighbouring classes always overlap (${F('overlap')}a). Overlap is also increased by visual scoring error near the limits and by plant-to-plant variation within a plot. The literature shows the same pattern: severity classes are harder to separate than healthy vs diseased — 70% vs 87% accuracy for southern corn rust (Meng et al., 2020), 81.8% vs 93.1% for southern corn leaf blight (Lv et al., 2023) — and adjacent rice-blast levels could not be separated by JM distance (Mandal et al., 2023).`));
K.push(P(`**How the methodology deals with it.** Overlap cannot be removed, but its effect is reduced and measured at every stage (${T('overlaptab')}).`));
K.push(...tab2('overlaptab', [
  ['Stage', 'What is done', 'Where (step)', 'Evidence from the papers'],
  ['Ground truth', 'Five sampling points per plot, same trained scorer, explicit class limits (0 < DI ≤ 25, 25 < DI ≤ 50, DI > 50) to reduce label noise near the limits', 'A2', 'Rater error in visual severity (Bock et al., 2010); DI from a multi-point survey (Liu et al., 2024)'],
  ['Feature screening', 'Correlation with continuous DI instead of class-separability thresholds, so no class limit is needed', '1.2, 1.3', 'Correlation with severity (Shukla et al., 2024; Guo et al., 2021); thresholds suit only non-overlapping stages (Tian et al., 2021)'],
  ['Disease-specific feature', 'White Rust Index built for white rust rather than general greenness', '1.4', 'Disease indices improved severity classification by 8.3% over the best published index (Meng et al., 2020; Mahlein et al., 2013)'],
  ['Multivariate separation', 'Discriminant analysis combines several features into functions that separate classes better than any single feature', '1.5', 'Stepwise discriminant analysis for mustard rot classes (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013); FLDA for three severity levels (Zhang et al., 2012a)'],
  ['Complementary information', 'Combining feature types and sensors (spectra, derivatives, texture, temperature) through ML-SFFS fusion and DNN fusion', '2.1, 2.3', 'Combining feature types improved severity classification by about 7% (Lv et al., 2023); fusion raised accuracy (Feng et al., 2022; Mahlein et al., 2019)'],
  ['Models suited to overlap', 'Soft-margin SVM (C tolerates overlapping samples), RF and k-NN; class weights for unequal classes', '2.2', 'Cortes and Vapnik (1995); Breiman (2001); macro-F1 and class weighting (Lv et al., 2023)'],
  ['Regression alongside classification', 'DI regression gives a continuous estimate; classes derived from predicted DI are compared with direct classification', '2.3, 2.4', 'DI regression of disease severity (Liu et al., 2024; Guo et al., 2021; Feng et al., 2022)'],
  ['Honest evaluation', 'Confusion matrix and per-class F1 show which neighbouring classes are confused; the share of errors between adjacent classes (± 1 class) is reported', '2.4', 'Per-class metrics for unequal classes (Sokolova and Lapalme, 2009)'],
], [1700, 3600, 900, 3106], 'How class overlap is handled at each stage of the methodology.', 15));
K.push(...fig2('overlap', 'm18_overlap.png', 'Dummy illustration: (a) neighbouring classes overlap for a single feature; (b) exact accuracy and accuracy within ± 1 class for direct classification and for classes derived from predicted DI.', 540));
K.push(KEY('Interview answer in one sentence:', `My classes are cuts of a continuous DI, so neighbouring classes overlap by nature; I reduce the effect with careful scoring, DI-based feature screening, a disease-specific index, multivariate discriminant analysis and multi-sensor fusion, I model DI by regression alongside the classes, and I report per-class F1 and adjacent-class errors so the overlap is visible rather than hidden (Meng et al., 2020; Lv et al., 2023; Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013).`));
// ======================= 2 COMMON STEPS =======================
K.push(H1('2. Common steps (used by all objectives)'));
FSTEP({ title: 'Step A1. Field experiment',
 pos: `First step of the thesis; its plots and treatments are used by every later step.`,
 concept: `A factorial randomised block design (RBD) applies every treatment combination once in each replication, randomised within the block, so that field gradients are removed and treatment effects can be compared fairly (Gomez and Gomez, 1984).`,
 need: `All later analyses need a set of plots that covers healthy and all severity levels on the same flight dates, with replications that can later serve as independent test sets.`,
 just: `Four inoculation timings on staggered sowings create different severities on every flight date; three varieties of different susceptibility widen the range and test robustness across genotypes; un-inoculated controls give the Healthy class at every date; three replications give three balanced validation folds. Field-plot designs with controls and repeated flights are standard in UAV disease studies (Guo et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2024).`,
 papers: [`Guo et al. (2021): plot experiment with inoculated and control plots, six UAV hyperspectral flights (wheat yellow rust).`, `Liu et al. (2024): 60 plots flown on five dates with UAV multispectral and thermal cameras (wheat powdery mildew).`, `Feng et al. (2022): two field experiments, the second used for independent validation.`],
 how: [`Factors: inoculation timing G1–G4 (sowing on 20 Nov, 30 Nov, 10 Dec and 20 Dec 2025; one inoculation on 10 Jan 2026), variety V1 Varuna (highly susceptible), V2 Pusa Vijay (moderately susceptible), V3 PM 38 (resistant), dose D1 control and D2 inoculated (1.5 × 10^{5} zoosporangia mL^{−1}).`,
       `24 treatments × 3 replications = 72 plots of 3 m × 3 m; uniform irrigation and fertiliser so that water or nutrient stress does not mimic disease.`],
 mine: `This is the IARI 2025–26 experiment of the ORW; the Bharatpur experiment and farmers' fields (2026–27) are used only for independent validation (Step 2.6).`,
 vis: [FIG('layout', 'm1_layout.png', 'Dummy randomisation of the 72 plots in three replications (24 treatments each).', 560)],
 out: `72 plots with known treatment, variety, inoculation timing and replication; a range of severities on every flight date.`,
 notes: [`**Split-plot design** when one factor (e.g. sowing date) cannot be randomised in small plots (Gomez and Gomez, 1984).`, `**Natural infection** at hot-spot locations or spreader rows (Saharan et al., 2014).`, `**Multi-season experiments** to capture year effects (Tian et al., 2021; Mustafa et al., 2023).`],
 qs: [[`Why include the resistant variety PM 38?`, `It gives inoculated plots with little or no disease, widens the host response and tests whether models confuse inoculation with disease.`], [`Why three replications?`, `They remove field gradients and give three balanced, independent test folds for leave-one-replication-out validation.`]],
 refs: `Gomez and Gomez (1984); Guo et al. (2021); Liu et al. (2024); Saharan et al. (2014).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step A2. Ground truth: disease index and classes',
 pos: `After Step A1 (plots exist); before every model, because all tasks are trained and tested against these labels.`,
 concept: `The disease index (DI) summarises leaf scores of a plot as a percentage of the maximum possible score (Liu et al., 2024):`,
 eqs: [EQ('DI (%) = [ Σ (x × f) / (n × Σ f) ] × 100', 'e:di')],
 need: `Supervised learning needs a reference value for every plot-date; classes and DI must be defined before features are related to disease.`,
 just: `The 0–6 leaf-area scale and the DI formula are standard for foliar diseases (Liu et al., 2024; Guo et al., 2021). Four classes give management levels (no action, monitor, treat, urgent), and DI regression keeps the full information. Writing the limits as 0 < DI ≤ 25, 25 < DI ≤ 50 and DI > 50 puts every value in exactly one class.`,
 papers: [`Liu et al. (2024): disease index from a five-point leaf survey per plot — the formula used here.`, `Guo et al. (2021): plot disease index as the regression target.`, `Shukla et al. (2024): severity grade 1–5 for mustard aphid; Mandal et al. (2023): IRRI 0–9 scale for rice blast; Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): % incidence grouped into levels.`],
 how: [`At the centre and four corners of each plot, fully expanded leaves are scored 0–6 by pustule cover (0 none; 1 0–5%; 2 5–10%; 3 10–20%; 4 20–35%; 5 35–50%; 6 > 50%); x = score, f = number of leaves with that score, n = 6.`,
       `Classes: Healthy (DI = 0), Low (0 < DI ≤ 25), Moderate (25 < DI ≤ 50), High (DI > 50). Physiological variables (chlorophyll a, b, carotenoid, anthocyanin, relative water content, stomatal conductance, transpiration) are measured to explain sensor responses.`],
 mine: `Example: 2 leaves at score 0, 3 at 1, 3 at 3 and 2 at 5 give DI = 22/60 × 100 = 36.7%, the Moderate class (${T('diex')}).`,
 vis: [TAB('diex', [['Score x', '0', '1', '3', '5', 'Σ'], ['Leaves f', '2', '3', '3', '2', '10'], ['x × f', '0', '3', '9', '10', '22']], [1800, 1300, 1300, 1300, 1300, 1300], 'Worked DI example: DI = 22/(6 × 10) × 100 = 36.7% (Moderate).', 18),
       FIG('di', 'm2_di.png', 'Dummy distribution of DI with the class limits (a) and the unequal class sizes (b).', 540)],
 out: `For every plot-date: DI (%), class (Healthy/Low/Moderate/High) and physiological measurements; class counts (unequal, so class weights are needed later).`,
 notes: [`**Image-based severity** from close-range images (Bock et al., 2010).`, `**AUDPC** for a season-level value (Shaner and Finney, 1977).`, `**Ordinal regression** of the scores instead of classes (McCullagh, 1980).`],
 qs: [[`Why include Healthy as a class?`, `Then one model does detection and severity together; healthy vs diseased accuracy is read from the confusion matrix.`], [`Why both classification and regression?`, `Classes are needed for decisions; DI regression keeps the continuous information and is less sensitive to plots near the class limits.`]],
 refs: `Liu et al. (2024); Guo et al. (2021); Bock et al. (2010).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step A3. UAV data acquisition and pre-processing',
 pos: `In parallel with Step A2 on each flight date; before Step 1.1, which extracts features from the calibrated products.`,
 concept: `Raw sensor signals must be converted into physically meaningful, comparable quantities — reflectance for RGB, MS and HS, temperature for TIR — and restricted to canopy pixels of each plot.`,
 need: `Without calibration, values from different flights and illumination conditions cannot be pooled; without canopy masks, soil and shadow dilute the disease signal.`,
 just: `Empirical-line calibration with panels is the standard UAV method (Smith and Milton, 1999; Aasen et al., 2018); the TIR camera is converted with emissivity and meteorological data and checked against ground targets (Maimaitijiang et al., 2020); the HS plot spectrum is the average of crop endmembers, which removes residual soil–canopy mixing (Keshava and Mustard, 2002; Winter, 1999); the canopy mask combines an NDVI threshold, which separates vegetation from soil (Guo et al., 2021), with the spectral angle mapper (SAM), which compares the shape of each pixel spectrum with a reference canopy spectrum independently of brightness and so rejects shadow, soil and non-canopy pixels with similar NDVI (Kruse et al., 1993); removing atmospheric water bands and Savitzky–Golay smoothing follow Mahlein et al. (2013) and Shukla et al. (2024).`,
 papers: [`Guo et al. (2021): NDVI mask to keep canopy pixels of UAV hyperspectral images.`, `Feng et al. (2022): K-means segmentation of canopy in thermal and RGB images.`, `Maimaitijiang et al. (2020): radiometric calibration of the FLIR thermal camera with ground targets.`, `Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): mean of 3 × 3 pixels as the spectrum of each observation.`],
 how: [`Flights at 30 m, 85% forward and 80% side overlap, 11:00–14:00 h, GNSS ground control points; structure-from-motion orthomosaics.`,
       `MS/HS: panels and irradiance sensor → reflectance (ρ = a·DN + b); HS: remove 1350–1450 and 1800–1950 nm and noisy edges; Savitzky–Golay smoothing.`,
       `TIR: 15–20 min warm-up; radiometric conversion (emissivity ≈ 0.98, air temperature, humidity, height).`,
       `Canopy mask on the HS image: a pixel is canopy if NDVI > 0.6 **and** its spectral angle to a reference canopy spectrum (mean of pure canopy pixels) is < 0.3 rad (SAM, Eq. [e:sam]); the thresholds are checked on every flight, especially at flowering, when yellow flowers lower NDVI.`,
       `Mask transfer: the HS mask is co-registered with the RGB, MS and TIR orthomosaics (same GCPs), resampled to each sensor's grid by nearest neighbour and eroded by one pixel at canopy edges, so that mixed edge pixels and small registration errors (warm soil in TIR, bright soil in RGB) are excluded.`,
       `HS plot spectrum: on the masked canopy pixels of each plot (0.5 m inner buffer), endmember extraction (e.g. N-FINDR or VCA) gives 30 candidate endmembers; endmembers that are not crop (soil, shadow, background: low NDVI or large spectral angle to the reference crop spectrum) are removed, and the remaining crop endmembers are averaged into one spectrum per plot-date. Chlorotic or pustule-covered crop endmembers are kept, because they carry the disease signal.`,
       `**Flower pixels — removed from the leaf spectrum, recorded separately.** Each of the 30 endmembers is labelled green crop, chlorotic/pustule crop, flower, soil or shadow; flower endmembers (high reflectance at 500–650 nm relative to blue, high NDYI = (R_{G} − R_{B})/(R_{G} + R_{B}); Sulik and Long, 2016) are removed together with soil and shadow, and the flower fraction of the plot (flower endmembers or yellow pixels / canopy) is stored as a covariate. For the staghead analysis after flowering, inflorescence pixels are kept, because stagheads are diseased inflorescences.`,
       `RGB, MS and TIR: mean of the masked canopy pixels inside the 0.5 m inner buffer, in each sensor's native resolution; the NDVI > 0.6 mask already excludes most flower-dominated pixels.`],
 eqs: [EQ('α = cos^{−1} [ Σ t_{i} r_{i} / ( √(Σ t_{i}^{2}) √(Σ r_{i}^{2}) ) ]', 'e:sam')],
 mine: `One canopy mask per flight from the HS image (NDVI > 0.6 and SAM < 0.3 rad) is applied to all four products; the HS spectrum of each plot is the average of its leaf-canopy endmembers (green and chlorotic/pustule crop) from the 30 extracted, with flower, soil and shadow endmembers removed (${T('endm')}); α is the angle between the pixel spectrum t and the reference spectrum r over all bands. The SWIR pixels (about 36 cm) leave about 5 × 5 pure pixels per plot, enough for a plot mean.`,
 vis: [FIG('preproc', 'm3_preproc.png', 'Pre-processing chain of each sensor.', 560), TAB('endm', [['Endmember class (dummy plot)', 'Number of 30', 'How identified', 'Decision'], ['Green crop', '17', 'High NDVI; small spectral angle to reference crop spectrum', 'Keep'], ['Chlorotic / pustule-covered crop', '6', 'Vegetation spectrum with higher visible reflectance; small–moderate angle', 'Keep (disease signal)'], ['Flower (yellow petals)', '4', 'High 500–650 nm reflectance, high NDYI, lower NDVI', 'Remove from leaf spectrum; count as flower fraction (4/27 of canopy endmembers)'], ['Soil', '2', 'Low NDVI; large spectral angle', 'Remove'], ['Shadow', '1', 'Very low reflectance at all wavelengths', 'Remove']], [2300, 1200, 3300, 2506], 'Dummy example of endmember classification for one plot: the plot spectrum is the mean of the 23 leaf-canopy endmembers.', 16)],
 out: `Calibrated reflectance (RGB, MS, HS) and canopy-temperature maps, canopy masks, and plot polygons ready for feature extraction.`,
 notes: [`**Radiative-transfer atmospheric correction** (6S) for higher altitudes or satellites (Vermote et al., 1997).`, `**Keeping flowers and modelling them**: estimate flower fraction and vegetation fraction separately from UAV data (Fang et al., 2016) and include flower fraction as a model input.`, `**Plot spectrum alternatives**: simple mean or median of masked canopy pixels, percentile spectra (Liu et al., 2024), the mean of 3 × 3 pixels (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013) or abundance maps from linear spectral unmixing (Keshava and Mustard, 2002).`, `**SNV/MSC scatter correction** of spectra (Barnes et al., 1989; Ramalingam et al., 2026).`, `**Sensor-specific masks** instead of one transferred mask: K-means on RGB and thermal images (Feng et al., 2022), Otsu thresholding (Otsu, 1979) or the excess-green index for RGB (Woebbecke et al., 1995).`],
 qs: [[`Why no full atmospheric correction?`, `At 30 m the atmospheric path is short; panels on every flight correct the remaining effect (Aasen et al., 2018).`], [`Why combine NDVI with SAM for the mask?`, `NDVI separates vegetation from soil but depends on brightness and drops at flowering; SAM compares spectral shape with a reference canopy spectrum, independently of illumination, so together they reject soil, shadow and mixed pixels (Kruse et al., 1993).`], [`Is it correct to apply the HS mask to RGB, MS and thermal images?`, `Yes, if the images are co-registered with the same GCPs; the mask is resampled to each grid and eroded by one pixel at edges so that registration errors and mixed edge pixels, especially warm soil in thermal images, are excluded. One mask also makes all sensors describe exactly the same canopy area.`], [`Why use endmembers instead of the mean of all canopy pixels?`, `Endmembers are the purest spectra in the plot, so averaging only the crop endmembers removes the remaining soil–canopy mixing that a simple mean keeps, which matters for the coarse SWIR pixels (Keshava and Mustard, 2002). Because endmembers are extreme spectra, I also report the simple masked mean as a check.`], [`How do you make sure diseased pixels are not removed as non-crop?`, `Non-crop endmembers are identified only by low NDVI or a large spectral angle to the reference crop spectrum (soil, shadow, background); chlorotic or pustule-covered canopy endmembers still have vegetation spectra and are kept.`], [`Do you keep or remove flower pixels?`, `I remove them from the leaf spectrum, because DI is scored on leaves and yellow petals dominate the 500–650 nm region; flowering also differs with sowing date and variety, so keeping flowers would mix phenology with disease (Sulik and Long, 2016; Fang et al., 2016). I store the flower fraction as a covariate and check that it is not what the models learn. For staghead severity, inflorescence pixels are kept because stagheads are the diseased organ.`], [`Which endmember algorithm do you use?`, `A geometric pure-pixel method such as N-FINDR (Winter, 1999) or VCA (Nascimento and Bioucas-Dias, 2005), with 30 endmembers per plot.`], [`Why 0.6 and 0.3 rad?`, `They are set by inspecting the NDVI and spectral-angle histograms of canopy and soil pixels and are checked on every flight; the sensitivity of plot means to small threshold changes is reported.`]],
 refs: `Smith and Milton (1999); Aasen et al. (2018); Maimaitijiang et al. (2020); Kruse et al. (1993); Winter (1999); Nascimento and Bioucas-Dias (2005); Keshava and Mustard (2002); Sulik and Long (2016); Fang et al. (2016); Guo et al. (2021); Mahlein et al. (2013).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step A4. Pooled dataset and validation design',
 pos: `After Steps A2 and A3 (labels and features per plot-date); it defines the training and test data used by every step of Objectives 1–3.`,
 concept: `All flights are pooled into one table with one row per plot-date. Model performance is estimated by **leave-one-replication-out (LORO)** cross-validation: train on two replications, test on the third, three times. Every data-driven choice (feature selection, tuning) is repeated inside the training replications (nested cross-validation).`,
 need: `It must be fixed before any selection is done, otherwise test plots would influence the selection and the accuracy would be optimistic.`,
 just: `Pooling gives all severity levels in one model (Liu et al., 2024). Dates of the same plot are dependent, so all dates of a plot stay in the same fold (Roberts et al., 2017); selection outside the folds causes selection bias (Ambroise and McLachlan, 2002; Varma and Simon, 2006).`,
 papers: [`Liu et al. (2024): one model for all five flight dates (pooled).`, `Tian et al. (2021): 4-fold cross-validation repeated 100 times; Guo et al. (2021): leave-one-out cross-validation.`, `Feng et al. (2022): validation on an independent experiment; Shukla et al. (2024): random 80/20 split.`],
 how: [`Outer loop: R1, R2, R3 held out in turn. Inner loop: grouped 4-fold CV by plot inside the two training replications for band choice (scalogram, ReliefF), Boruta, VIF, ML-SFFS and tuning.`, `Report mean ± SD over the three test replications; refit the final model on all IARI data.`],
 mine: `Images of all four sensors were acquired on **8 flight dates**, giving 72 × 8 = **576 plot-dates**. Dates before the first visible symptoms form the pre-symptomatic set (Objective 3) and the remaining dates the post-symptomatic set (Objectives 1 and 2); for example, 2 + 6 dates give 144 + 432 plot-dates (the real split depends on when symptoms appeared). Each plot contributes at most 8 correlated observations, which is why all its dates stay in one fold. With 6 post-symptomatic dates, each test fold has 24 plots × 6 = 144 plot-dates and each training set 48 × 6 = 288.`,
 vis: [FIG('cv', 'm4_cv.png', 'Leave-one-replication-out validation with grouped inner cross-validation.', 540)],
 out: `The pooled table (features + DI + class + plot ID + replication) and the fixed fold assignment.`,
 notes: [`**Spatial block CV** with distance-based blocks (Roberts et al., 2017).`, `**Leave-one-date-out** or date-wise models (Guo et al., 2021).`, `**.632+ bootstrap** error estimate (Efron and Tibshirani, 1997).`],
 qs: [[`Why not a random 80/20 split?`, `The same plot would appear in training and test on different dates, so the model could recognise the plot rather than the disease.`], [`What is nested cross-validation?`, `An inner loop for selection and tuning inside the training data and an outer loop for the performance estimate.`]],
 refs: `Liu et al. (2024); Roberts et al. (2017); Ambroise and McLachlan (2002); Varma and Simon (2006).` });

// ======================= 3 OBJECTIVE 1 =======================
K.push(H1('3. Objective 1: Characterise white rust through multimodal UAV sensing'));
K.push(P(`Objective 1 answers: which features of each sensor change with white rust, how strongly they separate the four classes and track DI, and which plant property explains the change. Its output — disease-specific feature (DSF) pools for classification and regression — is the input of Objectives 2 and 3.`));
FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.1. Feature extraction for each sensor',
 pos: `After Step A3 (calibrated canopy pixels); before Steps 1.2–1.6, which analyse these features.`,
 concept: `Each sensor's pixels are summarised per plot into features that describe a physical property: reflectance, derivatives and indices (pigments, structure, water), texture (spatial pattern) and temperature indices (transpiration).`,
 need: `Statistics and models work on a table of plot-level features, not on images.`,
 just: `Features follow the reference papers: narrow-band indices and derivatives (Guo et al., 2021; Tian et al., 2021; Mahlein et al., 2013), MS indices (Liu et al., 2024), GLCM texture (Haralick et al., 1973; Feng et al., 2022), thermal indices CT, CTD, CTR, NRCT and MTD (Feng et al., 2022; Mahlein et al., 2019), canopy cover and height (Maimaitijiang et al., 2020). The 39 hyperspectral indices include all indices of the ORW slides plus disease-related indices from the papers (${T('hsidx')}).`,
 papers: [`Guo et al. (2021): 15 narrow-band indices + GLCM texture from UAV hyperspectral data.`, `Liu et al. (2024): 12 multispectral indices, texture and canopy temperature.`, `Feng et al. (2022): vegetation indices, five texture measures and CT, CTD, CTR, NRCT.`, `Mahlein et al. (2019): PSSRa/b and thermal MTD; Maimaitijiang et al. (2020): spectral, structural, thermal and texture features.`],
 how: [`HS: 481 band reflectances, first and second derivatives (Savitzky–Golay), red-edge position REP, amplitude Dr and area SDr, 39 indices (${T('hsidx')}); the White Rust Index is added in Step 1.4.`, `MS: 5 bands, 12 indices (${T('msidx')}), 40 GLCM textures (8 per band).`, `RGB: 24 GLCM textures (8 per band), mean hue, saturation, value, vegetation fraction, canopy height (29 features).`, `TIR: CT, CTD = CT − AT, CTR (in kelvin), NRCT = (CT − CT_{min})/(CT_{max} − CT_{min}), MTD, 8 GLCM textures (13 features).`],
 eqs: [EQ('REP = argmax_{680–750 nm} FD(λ);   CTD = CT − AT;   NRCT = (CT − CT_{min}) / (CT_{max} − CT_{min})', 'e:feat')],
 mine: `HS gives 1443 band-level features plus 3 red-edge parameters and 39 indices; MS 57; RGB 29; TIR 13. ROTI uses 1982 nm, next to the removed water band, so it is computed only if that band passes the noise check.`,
 vis: [TAB('hsidx', __HSIDX__), TAB('msidx', __MSIDX__), FIG('spectra', 'm5_spectra.png', 'Dummy class-mean reflectance and first-derivative spectra of the four classes: visible reflectance rises, the red edge shifts and the NIR falls with severity.', 560)],
 out: `One feature table per sensor (rows = plot-dates): HS 1485 + WRI, MS 57, RGB 29, TIR 13 features.`,
 notes: [`**Continuous wavelet features** (Tian et al., 2021; Lv et al., 2023).`, `**Continuum-removed band depths** (Kokaly and Clark, 1999).`, `**PROSAIL inversion** to retrieve chlorophyll, water and LAI (Jacquemoud et al., 2009).`, `**Three-band texture indices** (Liu et al., 2024).`],
 qs: [[`Why so many hyperspectral indices?`, `They are the indices of my ORW plus disease-related indices from the papers; Boruta and VIF later keep only the relevant, non-redundant ones.`], [`Why compute CTR in kelvin?`, `A ratio of Celsius values depends on the zero of the scale; in kelvin it is physically meaningful.`]],
 refs: `Guo et al. (2021); Liu et al. (2024); Feng et al. (2022); Haralick et al. (1973); Mahlein et al. (2019); Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.2. Univariate response of every feature to the four classes',
 pos: `After Step 1.1 (feature tables); it ranks features for interpretation and runs in parallel with Steps 1.3–1.5. Its results explain the DSFs selected in Step 1.6.`,
 concept: `Each feature is compared across Healthy, Low, Moderate and High with **one-way ANOVA**, **Tukey's HSD test** shows which pairs of classes differ, **Pearson's r** measures how strongly the feature changes with DI, and correlation with physiological variables explains why it changes.`,
 need: `Before multivariate selection, one must know which features respond, in which direction and between which classes; this is the physiological characterisation required by Objective 1.`,
 just: `ANOVA with Tukey HSD is the standard test for comparing several treatment groups in agricultural research (Gomez and Gomez, 1984; Tukey, 1949); Pearson's r is the simplest measure of association with DI. Statistical characterisation of features in this way is common in disease studies (Zhang et al., 2012a; Mahlein et al., 2019; Liu et al., 2024).`,
 papers: [`Mahlein et al. (2019): Tukey HSD between groups and Pearson r with disease severity.`, `Liu et al. (2024): Dunnett test of features between disease levels.`, `Zhang et al. (2012a): t-test and correlation of 32 spectral features with severity.`, `Guo et al. (2021): features kept if significantly correlated with DI (p < 0.01).`],
 how: [`One-way ANOVA of each feature across the four classes; Tukey HSD for pairs (letters a, b, c mark classes that differ).`, `Pearson r with DI; correlation with chlorophyll, carotenoid, anthocyanin, RWC, stomatal conductance and transpiration.`, `Thermal check: warmer diseased plots should not have lower water status (CTD vs WI/DWSI-1 and RWC), so that warming is attributed to disease, not drought (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013).`, `p-values are used only to rank features, because repeated plot-dates are not independent.`],
 mine: `One results table per sensor; ${T('statsres')} and ${F('box')} show the format with dummy data.`,
 vis: [TAB('statsres', __STATS__), FIG('box', 'm6_box.png', 'Dummy boxplots of one feature per sensor across the four classes, with the ANOVA F, Tukey letters and Pearson r with DI.', 560)],
 out: `Per sensor: mean ± SD of every feature per class, ANOVA F, Tukey letters, Pearson r with DI and the physiological correlates.`,
 notes: [`**Kruskal–Wallis + Dunn test**, the rank-based version if a feature is strongly non-normal (Kruskal and Wallis, 1952; Dunn, 1964).`, `**Spearman's ρ** for clearly non-linear relations with DI (Spearman, 1904; Shukla et al., 2024).`, `**Linear mixed models** with plot as a random effect for repeated measures (Zuur et al., 2009).`],
 qs: [[`Why ANOVA and not a t-test?`, `There are four classes; a t-test compares only two groups. ANOVA tests all four together and Tukey HSD then shows which pairs differ.`], [`Are your p-values valid with repeated plot-dates?`, `They are optimistic, so I use them only to describe and rank features; model accuracy on held-out replications is the real test.`]],
 refs: `Gomez and Gomez (1984); Tukey (1949); Zhang et al. (2012a); Liu et al. (2024).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.3. Hyperspectral band-level analysis',
 pos: `After Step 1.1 (HS band features), HS only; before Steps 1.4 (ReliefF ranking reused for the White Rust Index), 1.5 (band step of the discriminant analysis) and 1.6 (Boruta).`,
 concept: `Two complementary band rankings are made. A **correlation scalogram** plots Pearson's r between DI and the reflectance (and first and second derivative) at every wavelength, showing which regions change steadily with severity. **ReliefF** gives every band a weight for how well it separates neighbouring samples of different classes (Healthy, Low, Moderate, High). Neighbouring bands are almost identical, so from each list only **one band per correlated region** is kept.`,
 need: `1443 band features cannot enter a discriminant analysis or a model directly: they are redundant and outnumber the plots.`,
 just: `The severity classes are cuts of a continuous DI and overlap. A DI-based correlation needs no class limits and serves DI regression; ReliefF is designed for noisy, overlapping, multi-class data and serves classification (Kononenko, 1994; Robnik-Šikonja and Kononenko, 2003). Separability scalograms (Tian et al., 2021) need two well-separated classes, SPA (Zhu et al., 2017) selects bands for low collinearity rather than disease response, and PCA loadings follow overall spectral variance rather than disease, so they fit this situation less well. Keeping one band per region follows Tian et al. (2021) and Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).`,
 papers: [`Shukla et al. (2024): correlation of reflectance, first and second derivatives with aphid grade in mustard (sensitive regions 483–487, 509–515, 690–714, 719–723 nm).`, `Guo et al. (2021): correlation of spectral features with DI; Mandal et al. (2022): correlation above 0.6 in 550–760 and 1140–1300 nm for rice blast.`, `Mahlein et al. (2013): ReliefF ranking of bands for disease indices; Meng et al. (2020): ReliefF for detection and three-level severity of corn rust; Lv et al. (2023): ReliefF + SFFS for severity classification.`, `Tian et al. (2021): separability scalogram with one band per feature region.`],
 how: [`Scalogram: r(λ) for R, FD and SD vs DI; bands with p < 0.01 and in the top 5% of |r| are candidates (Guo et al., 2021; Tian et al., 2021).`, `ReliefF: k = 10 neighbours, four classes, neighbours from the same plot excluded; top 5% of weights are candidates.`, `Correlated regions (both lists): from the band-to-band correlation matrix, a region is a run of neighbouring bands correlating with its first band at r ≥ 0.95; from each region only the band with the highest |r| (scalogram) or weight (ReliefF) is kept.`, `Combining the lists: a region found by both methods (same region, or within ±10 nm) is a **common** band → robust white-rust wavelength, used for both tasks; scalogram-only bands → DI regression pool; ReliefF-only bands → classification pool. Boruta, VIF and ML-SFFS later remove what is not needed.`, `When bands become model inputs, this is repeated inside the training replications.`, `**Vegetation indices are not screened in this step.** Step 1.3 deals only with the raw bands (481 R, FD and SD), which are redundant and too many. The 39 indices, red-edge parameters and WRI are few, physically defined and go directly to Step 1.2 (ANOVA, r with DI), Step 1.5 (second discriminant step) and Step 1.6 (Boruta, together with the robust bands).`],
 mine: `In my ASD mustard leaf spectra (3 March 2026, 648 spectra), bands 10 nm apart have median r = 0.9997 and 349 bands form only 18 correlated regions (${F('bandcorr')}); a similar reduction is expected for the UAV spectra.`,
 vis: [FIG('scal', 'm7_scalogram.png', 'Dummy correlation scalograms (reflectance and first derivative vs DI) and ReliefF weights; red marks the top 5%.', 560), TAB('bandgroups', [['Group', 'Dummy example (one band per region)', 'Meaning', 'Use'], ['Common to both', '558, 705, 1650 nm', 'Changes steadily with DI and separates classes', 'Robust white-rust bands; both classification and regression pools'], ['Scalogram only', '760, 2200 nm', 'Steady trend with DI, weak class separation', 'Regression (DI) pool'], ['ReliefF only', '1450–1500 region (1495 nm)', 'Class-specific or non-linear response', 'Classification (4-class) pool']], [1700, 2400, 2700, 2506], 'Dummy example of combining the two band lists after keeping one band per correlated region.', 16), FIG('bandcorr', 'n8_bandcorr.png', 'Real example of band redundancy from the mustard ASD leaf spectra: (a) band-to-band correlation of reflectance, (b) of first derivatives, (c) correlation with bands 5–50 nm apart.', 560)],
 out: `Robust white-rust bands (common to both methods), a DI-regression band list and a classification band list, each with one band per correlated region (typically 10–20 bands in total).`,
 notes: [`**Separability scalograms** for two well-separated classes (Tian et al., 2021).`, `**SPA or CARS** wavelength selection (Araújo et al., 2001; Li et al., 2009).`, `**PCA/PLSR loading weights** (Shukla et al., 2024) or **PLS VIP scores** (Wold et al., 2001).`, `**Intersection only**: keep only common regions, as Shukla et al. (2024) kept regions common to R, FD and SD — simpler, but task-specific bands are lost.`],
 qs: [[`Why not use all 481 bands?`, `Neighbouring bands are near-duplicates (r ≈ 1); using all would overfit and make discriminant analysis impossible.`], [`Why both the scalogram and ReliefF?`, `The scalogram finds bands that change steadily with DI (regression); ReliefF finds bands that separate the four classes, including non-linear responses (classification). Bands found by both are the most robust.`], [`Are vegetation indices also selected by the scalogram and ReliefF?`, `No. That step is only for raw bands; the 39 indices are tested in Step 1.2 and enter the discriminant analysis and Boruta directly, together with the robust bands.`], [`Do you keep only bands common to both?`, `Common bands are my key white-rust wavelengths and go to both tasks; scalogram-only bands go to DI regression and ReliefF-only bands to classification, because a band can matter for one task only.`], [`Why not Tian's separability scalogram, SPA or PCA loadings?`, `Separability thresholds need two well-separated classes, SPA picks bands for low collinearity, and PCA follows overall spectral variance; my classes overlap and I need bands that respond to disease.`]],
 refs: `Shukla et al. (2024); Guo et al. (2021); Robnik-Šikonja and Kononenko (2003); Mahlein et al. (2013); Meng et al. (2020); Tian et al. (2021).` });

K.push(H3('Why a correlation scalogram + ReliefF, and not other band-selection methods'));
K.push(P(`Correlation scalogram + ReliefF is the right pair for this data because the problem has three features that the alternatives were not designed for: (i) **overlapping, ordered classes** — Healthy, Low, Moderate and High are cut from a continuous DI, so neighbouring classes overlap; (ii) **two tasks** — bands are needed for both classification and DI regression; (iii) **characterisation** — Objective 1 asks which wavelengths respond to white rust, so the method must give an interpretable relevance value for every band.`));
K.push(...tab2('altband', [
  ['Alternative', 'How it works', 'Why it suits the original study', 'Why it fits this data less well'],
  ['Separability scalogram (Tian et al., 2021)', 'For each band the gap between healthy and infected means is divided into 100 thresholds; the best threshold accuracy is the band\'s separability; top 5% kept', 'Two classes (healthy vs infected) per day after inoculation, leaves under controlled conditions, clean separation', 'One threshold per band cannot split four ordered classes; near 25% and 50% Low/Moderate and Moderate/High overlap, so all bands score poorly; ignores continuous DI (no help for regression). Kept in Notes for a healthy-vs-diseased check'],
  ['Successive projections algorithm, SPA (Zhu et al., 2017; Huang et al., 2020)', 'Picks wavelengths with minimum collinearity by vector projections; the number kept is chosen by the error of multiple linear regression', 'Building a small, non-redundant wavelength set for a calibration model or a low-cost multispectral device', 'Aims at non-redundancy, not disease response (a band may be chosen only because it is uncorrelated); depends on the starting wavelength; tied to linear MLR; gives a band combination, not a relevance value per band, so no "response spectrum" for Objective 1. Collinearity is handled here by one band per correlated region + VIF'],
  ['PCA loading weights (Shukla et al., 2024)', 'Bands at peaks and troughs of the loadings of the first principal components', 'Reducing dimensionality of ground spectra', 'Unsupervised: components follow the largest spectral variance, which in UAV field data comes mainly from canopy structure, illumination, growth stage and flowering, not necessarily disease'],
  ['PLSR loading weights (Shukla et al., 2024; Krishna et al., 2014)', 'Bands at peaks and troughs of the loading weights of the first PLS factors (supervised with DI)', 'Selecting a few bands for a severity-prediction model', 'Each factor is a weighted sum of all bands; picking peaks by eye is subjective and depends on how many factors are inspected; hard to interpret — Shukla et al. noted their bands (500, 679, 746, 979 nm) were not linked to known pigment or water absorption bands. Their own first step for sensitive regions was correlation of R, FD and SD with grade, i.e. a correlation scalogram'],
], [1900, 2300, 2100, 3006], 'Alternative band-selection methods and why they fit this situation less well.', 15));
K.push(...tab2('whyscal', [
  ['Property of the correlation scalogram', 'Benefit for this data'],
  ['Uses continuous DI, no class limits', 'Works despite overlapping classes; serves the regression task directly'],
  ['One coefficient per band, plotted against wavelength', 'Easy to present and to explain physically: pigment (red), red edge, NIR structure, SWIR water'],
  ['Works on R, FD and SD', 'Shows where slopes change (red-edge shift), not only reflectance'],
  ['Paper basis', 'Shukla et al. (2024) on mustard; Guo et al. (2021) for UAV yellow rust; Mandal et al. (2022) for rice blast'],
  ['Limitation', 'Captures only a steady (linear or monotonic) relation with DI and looks at one band at a time — covered by ReliefF'],
], [3400, 5906], 'Why the correlation scalogram is used.', 16));
K.push(...tab2('whyrelief', [
  ['Property of ReliefF', 'Benefit for this data'],
  ['Designed for multi-class, noisy, overlapping data (Kononenko, 1994; Robnik-Šikonja and Kononenko, 2003)', 'Handles the four classes directly — exactly the situation that breaks the separability scalogram'],
  ['Judges a band by nearest neighbours: same-class "hits" vs other-class "misses"', 'Captures non-linear and class-specific responses and bands useful only in combination; no normality or linearity assumption'],
  ['Paper basis', 'Mahlein et al. (2013) for disease-specific indices; Meng et al. (2020) for detection and three-level severity of corn rust; Lv et al. (2023) ReliefF + SFFS for severity classification'],
  ['Limitation', 'Correlated neighbouring bands get similar weights — handled by one band per correlated region (r ≥ 0.95) and later VIF'],
], [3400, 5906], 'Why ReliefF is used alongside the scalogram.', 16));
K.push(P(`**How the two work together.** The scalogram (regression side) finds bands that change steadily with DI; ReliefF (classification side) finds bands that separate the four classes locally. Bands picked by both are the most robust white-rust bands. Because only one band per correlated region is kept, the output is a short, interpretable list (typically 10–20 bands).`));
K.push(H3('One band per correlated region, and which bands are kept'));
K.push(P(`**The rule applies to both lists**, as one step after both methods have run: (1) scalogram candidates — p < 0.01 and top 5% of |r| with DI, for R, FD and SD; (2) ReliefF candidates — top 5% of weights for the four classes; (3) grouping into correlated regions — from the band-to-band correlation matrix, a region is a run of neighbouring bands correlating with each other at r ≥ 0.95 (the mustard ASD data collapse 349 bands into 18 regions); (4) one band per region — the band with the highest |r| (scalogram) or the highest weight (ReliefF). Without this step both top-5% lists fill up with near-duplicate neighbours of the same feature (${T('regionex')}).`));
K.push(...tab2('regionex', [
  ['Method', 'Candidate bands', 'Correlated region', 'Band kept'],
  ['Scalogram', '690, 695, 700, 705 nm', '700–710 nm', 'The one with the highest |r|, e.g. 705 nm'],
  ['ReliefF', '698, 702 nm', '700–710 nm', '702 nm — both methods agree on region 700–710 nm'],
], [1800, 2500, 2000, 3006], 'Dummy example of keeping one band per correlated region in each list.', 17));
K.push(P(`**Common bands are not the only ones kept.** Bands found by both methods are reported as the key white-rust wavelengths and enter both tasks; scalogram-only bands enter the DI-regression pool and ReliefF-only bands the classification pool (${T('bandgroups')}). Keeping only the intersection would (i) leave too few bands (possibly 2–3) for some sensors or tasks and (ii) lose task-specific information — for example, a SWIR water band may separate High from Moderate (picked by ReliefF) without a linear trend over all DI values (missed by the scalogram). Keeping the task-specific bands is safe because Boruta, VIF and ML-SFFS later remove what is not needed.`));
K.push(P(`**When intersection only is acceptable.** For the simplest possible story, keeping only common regions is defensible: Shukla et al. (2024) kept regions common to reflectance, first and second derivatives, and Tian et al. (2021) kept features common to all stages. The cost is possible loss of task-specific information; because this methodology keeps separate pools for classification and regression, the task-by-task rule fits it better.`));
K.push(P(`**Two practical points.** (1) "Common" means the same correlated region, not the identical wavelength: 700 and 705 nm in the same r ≥ 0.95 region count as common (a ±10 nm window gives the same result). (2) When the bands become model inputs (Objectives 2 and 3), all of these steps are repeated on the training replications only.`));
K.push(KEY('Interview answers:', `**Why this pair?** "My severity classes are cuts of a continuous disease index and overlap, so I avoided threshold-based separability scalograms, which are built for two well-separated classes, and variance- or collinearity-driven methods like PCA loadings and SPA, which do not tell me which wavelengths respond to disease. I use a correlation scalogram against DI, as Shukla et al. (2024) did for mustard, for the continuous trend, and ReliefF, designed for noisy, overlapping multi-class data and used for disease severity by Mahlein et al. (2013) and Meng et al. (2020), for class separation; then I keep one band per correlated region." **Which bands do you keep?** "I keep one band per correlated region from each method; bands found by both methods are my robust white-rust wavelengths and go to both tasks, while scalogram-only bands go to DI regression and ReliefF-only bands to classification, and Boruta, VIF and ML-SFFS then remove what is not needed."`));
FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.4. White Rust Index (WRI)',
 pos: `After Step 1.3 (its ReliefF band ranking is reused); the WRI is added to the HS features used in Steps 1.5 and 1.6.`,
 concept: `A spectral disease index combines a normalised difference of two bands with a weighted third band, chosen by exhaustive search to separate healthy and diseased canopy best (Mahlein et al., 2013):`,
 eqs: [EQ('WRI = (R_{λ1} − R_{λ2}) / (R_{λ1} + R_{λ2}) + w R_{λ3},   w ∈ {−1, −0.5, 0, 0.5, 1}', 'e:wri')],
 need: `General indices also respond to nutrient or water stress; a disease-specific index is needed to characterise white rust itself.`,
 just: `Mahlein et al. (2013) built indices for three sugar-beet diseases with 85–92% balanced accuracy; disease indices also worked for corn rust (Meng et al., 2020), rice blast (Mandal et al., 2023) and mustard rot (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013, who found SWIR bands most sensitive).`,
 papers: [`Mahlein et al. (2013): ReliefF + exhaustive search of ND + wR; 85–92% balanced accuracy for three sugar-beet diseases.`, `Meng et al. (2020): ReliefF-based health and severity indices for southern corn rust.`, `Mandal et al. (2023): ratio and normalised-difference blast indices (R1148, R1301).`, `Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): three-band ROTI for mustard rot.`],
 how: [`Spectra averaged to 6 nm (450–950 nm); ReliefF keeps the top 25% of bands and of normalised differences; bands combined must be ≥ 50 nm apart (Mahlein et al., 2013).`, `Every combination is used as a one-feature classifier with an optimised threshold; criterion = balanced accuracy (Healthy vs diseased) in 8-fold cross-validation.`, `The search is repeated with SWIR bands added; the better index is kept; its correlation r with DI is also reported.`],
 mine: `In the dummy search the best pair was about ${DUM.wri_best[0]} and ${DUM.wri_best[1]} nm (balanced accuracy ${DUM.wri_best[2]}); the real bands come from the UAV data.`,
 vis: [FIG('wri', 'm8_wri.png', 'Dummy WRI search: balanced accuracy of normalised differences (a; star = best pair) and WRI values by class (b).', 560)],
 out: `The WRI formula (λ1, λ2, λ3, w, threshold), its balanced accuracy and its correlation with DI.`,
 notes: [`**Two-band RSI/DSI/NDSI searches** (Shukla et al., 2024).`, `**Separate health and severity indices** (Meng et al., 2020).`, `**Three-band ROTI-type indices** (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013).`],
 qs: [[`How is your WRI different from NDVI?`, `NDVI responds to any loss of greenness; the WRI is searched specifically to separate white-rust plots from healthy ones.`], [`Why a 50 nm minimum distance?`, `Neighbouring bands are almost identical, so their difference would carry little information (Mahlein et al., 2013).`]],
 refs: `Mahlein et al. (2013); Meng et al. (2020); Mandal et al. (2023); Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.5. Class discrimination: stepwise canonical discriminant analysis',
 pos: `After Steps 1.3 and 1.4 (robust bands and WRI); in parallel with Step 1.6. It describes how well each sensor separates the four classes and which features drive the separation.`,
 concept: `**Linear discriminant analysis** (LDA) finds combinations of features — discriminant functions — that separate the four classes as much as possible (Fisher, 1936). In the stepwise form, features are added one at a time and kept only if they improve the separation (measured by **Wilks' lambda**: 0 = classes fully separated, 1 = not separated); the **canonical correlation** (0–1) says how strongly a function is linked to the classes.`,
 need: `Univariate tests look at one feature at a time; discrimination between the four classes is a multivariate question.`,
 just: `This is Stage III of the mustard rot study of Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): a first stepwise analysis on band reflectances, then a second on indices, ranked by canonical correlation. Fisher LDA also discriminated powdery-mildew severity levels (Zhang et al., 2012a).`,
 papers: [`Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): two-step stepwise canonical discriminant analysis — 25 Hyperion bands, then indices (ROTI, DRI, DWSI) — for mustard rot classes.`, `Zhang et al. (2012a): Fisher LDA for three powdery-mildew severity levels (> 90% for heavy damage).`, `Tian et al. (2021): LDA as one of the ML-SFFS classifiers.`],
 how: [`HS: step 1 on the robust bands of Step 1.3; step 2 on indices, red-edge parameters and WRI (after VIF < 10). MS, RGB, TIR: one step on their features after VIF.`, `Features enter if they improve separation significantly (p < 0.05) and leave if they no longer do (p > 0.10); fitted in training replications; accuracy by LORO.`, `Outputs: the selected features, canonical correlation, the score plot of the classes and the cross-validated accuracy.`],
 mine: `With the dummy HS features the first function had canonical correlation ${DUM.cda.can[0]}, Wilks' lambda ${DUM.cda.wilks} and cross-validated accuracy ${DUM.cda.acc}; in the score plot Healthy and Low overlap while High is clearly separate.`,
 vis: [TAB('cda', __CDA__), FIG('cdaf', 'm9_cda.png', 'Dummy canonical score plot of the four classes (X = class centroid).', 420)],
 out: `For each sensor: the features that separate the classes, the canonical correlation, a score plot and the cross-validated accuracy (a simple linear benchmark for Objective 2).`,
 notes: [`**JM distance** between pairs of classes (Das et al., 2020; Mandal et al., 2023).`, `**PLS discriminant analysis** (Barker and Rayens, 2003).`, `**Regularised or quadratic discriminant analysis** (Friedman, 1989; Das et al., 2020).`, `**t-SNE/UMAP** visualisation (Ramalingam et al., 2026).`],
 qs: [[`What does Wilks' lambda mean?`, `The share of variance not explained by class differences; near 0 = well separated classes.`], [`Why is Healthy–Low hardest?`, `Low plots have few pustules, so their spectra are close to healthy; their points overlap in the score plot.`]],
 refs: `Fisher (1936); Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013); Zhang et al. (2012a).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 1.6. Disease-specific feature pools (Boruta) and cross-sensor comparison',
 pos: `After Steps 1.2–1.5; last step of Objective 1. Its DSF pools are the input of Step 2.1 (Objective 2) and Step 3.2 (Objective 3).`,
 concept: `**Boruta** adds a shuffled "shadow" copy of every feature, fits a random forest repeatedly and keeps a feature only if it is significantly more important than the best shadow (Kursa and Rudnicki, 2010). A **cross-sensor correlation matrix** shows whether sensors carry different information.`,
 need: `Objective 2 needs, for each sensor and task, a set of features that are truly related to white rust.`,
 just: `Boruta is all-relevant, captures non-linear effects and has a statistical cut-off; it was used to select disease-specific features before ML-SFFS by Mustafa et al. (2023). Low correlation between sensors justifies fusion (Mahlein et al., 2019).`,
 papers: [`Mustafa et al. (2023): Boruta to select disease-specific features before VIF and ML-SFFS.`, `Liu et al. (2024): recursive feature elimination; Feng et al. (2022): SPA.`, `Mahlein et al. (2019): correlation between thermal, fluorescence and spectral parameters to judge complementarity.`],
 how: [`Input per sensor: all features (HS: robust bands, red-edge parameters, indices, WRI).`, `Boruta twice per sensor: classification with the four classes and regression with DI (500 trees, up to 100 runs, p = 0.01).`, `Confirmed features = DSF pool; Pearson correlation among the DSFs of all sensors.`],
 mine: `Eight DSF pools: 4 sensors × (classification, regression). ${F('boruta')} shows a dummy MS result and ${F('xcorr')} a dummy cross-sensor matrix.`,
 vis: [FIG('boruta', 'm10_boruta.png', 'Dummy Boruta importance boxplots for the MS sensor (4-class task).', 560), FIG('xcorr', 'm11_xcorr.png', 'Dummy correlation matrix of DSFs from the four sensors.', 430)],
 out: `Eight DSF pools with confirmed/rejected features and importance plots, and the cross-sensor correlation matrix.`,
 notes: [`**Recursive feature elimination** (Guyon et al., 2002; Liu et al., 2024).`, `**LASSO/elastic net** (Tibshirani, 1996; Zou and Hastie, 2005).`, `**SHAP values** (Lundberg and Lee, 2017).`],
 qs: [[`What are shadow features?`, `Shuffled copies of the real features; they carry no information, so a real feature must beat the best of them to be confirmed.`], [`Why separate pools for classification and regression?`, `A feature can separate classes without tracking DI linearly, and vice versa.`]],
 refs: `Kursa and Rudnicki (2010); Mustafa et al. (2023); Mahlein et al. (2019).` });
K.push(BOX('How Objective 1 is satisfied:', `Steps 1.2–1.5 show which features of each sensor respond to white rust, how they change across Healthy, Low, Moderate and High and with DI, and why (physiology); Step 1.4 gives a white-rust-specific index; Step 1.6 gives the DSF pools and shows whether the sensors are complementary.`, 'E3F2FD', '1565C0'));

// ======================= 4 OBJECTIVE 2 =======================
K.push(H1('4. Objective 2: Severity classification and regression by multimodal fusion'));
FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.1. Optimum feature combination: VIF → ML-SFFS',
 pos: `After Step 1.6 (DSF pools); before Step 2.2, whose models are trained on the selected features. Done inside every training replication.`,
 concept: `The **variance inflation factor** VIF_{j} = 1/(1 − R_{j}^{2}) measures how well a feature is explained by the others; features are removed until all VIF < 10. **ML-SFFS** then adds the best feature at each step and removes earlier ones that become redundant, using the model's cross-validated score as the criterion (Pudil et al., 1994).`,
 eqs: [EQ('VIF_{j} = 1 / (1 − R_{j}^{2})', 'e:vif')],
 need: `DSF pools still contain collinear features (e.g. several NDVI variants), and models generalise better with a few strong features.`,
 just: `VIF < 10 followed by ML-SFFS was used by Tian et al. (2021) and Mustafa et al. (2023); 2–4 features were enough for rice blast and SVM-SFFS was up to 10% more accurate than SVM with all features (Tian et al., 2021).`,
 papers: [`Tian et al. (2021): VIF < 10 then ML-SFFS; 2–4 features enough.`, `Mustafa et al. (2023): Boruta → VIF < 10 → ML-SFFS across three sensors.`, `Zhu et al. (2017) and Huang et al. (2020): SPA wavelength selection.`],
 how: [`VIF: remove the largest VIF and recompute until all < 10.`, `ML-SFFS for each model: criterion macro-F1 (classification) or RMSE (regression); up to 10 features; grouped 4-fold CV inside training.`, `Optimum feature combination (OFC) = the subset with the best cross-validated score; if a smaller subset scores within 0.01 of it, the smaller subset is chosen. The search is repeated 10 times and features chosen in most repeats are kept.`],
 mine: `${T('viftab')} shows VIF removing NDVI780 and SR850 from a dummy HS pool; in the dummy ML-SFFS run (${F('sffs')}) SVM kept ${DUM.sffs.SVM[0].split(', ').length} features (macro-F1 ${DUM.sffs.SVM[1]}). RF kept one noise feature in a single run, which is why selection frequency over repeated runs is used.`,
 vis: [TAB('viftab', __VIF__), FIG('sffs', 'm12_sffs.png', 'Dummy ML-SFFS curves for SVM, k-NN and RF; star = optimum feature combination.', 520)],
 out: `For each sensor or sensor set, task and model: the OFC (typically 3–8 features) and its cross-validated score.`,
 notes: [`**SPA** (Araújo et al., 2001; Huang et al., 2020).`, `**Recursive feature elimination** (Guyon et al., 2002).`, `**mRMR** (Peng et al., 2005).`],
 qs: [[`Why VIF before SFFS?`, `Removing near-duplicate features first makes the search faster and more stable.`], [`What is "floating" in SFFS?`, `After adding a feature, earlier features are removed if that improves the score, so early wrong choices are corrected.`]],
 refs: `Pudil et al. (1994); Tian et al. (2021); Mustafa et al. (2023).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.2. Six models and their tuning',
 pos: `After Step 2.1 (OFC per model); the trained models are compared in Steps 2.3 and 2.4.`,
 concept: `Six models of different families: **SVM/SVR** (maximum-margin kernel method), **RF/RFR** (ensemble of decision trees), **k-NN** (majority or mean of nearest neighbours), **PLSR** (linear latent components, regression only), **DNN-F1** (all features concatenated at the input) and **DNN-F2** (one sub-network per sensor joined at an intermediate layer).`,
 need: `No single model is best for every sensor and task; comparing model families shows whether linear, kernel, tree or deep models suit white-rust data.`,
 just: `SVM was the best SFFS criterion in Tian et al. (2021); RFR was best in Feng et al. (2022); k-NN was best in Mustafa et al. (2023); PLSR is the standard linear baseline for spectra (Shukla et al., 2024; Guo et al., 2021); DNN-F2 was the best fusion model in Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).`,
 papers: [`Feng et al. (2022): PLSR, SVR and RFR — RFR best.`, `Mustafa et al. (2023): k-NN, RF and SVM — k-NN best for regression.`, `Maimaitijiang et al. (2020): PLSR, SVR, RFR, DNN-F1, DNN-F2 — DNN-F2 best.`, `Tian et al. (2021): SVM, k-NN, LDA; Shukla et al. (2024) and Guo et al. (2021): PLSR.`],
 how: [`SVM/SVR: RBF kernel, C and γ by grid search; class weights for unequal classes.`, `RF/RFR: 500 trees, mtry and leaf size tuned. k-NN: k = 3–11 on standardised features. PLSR: 1–10 components by minimum RMSE.`, `DNN-F1/F2: dense layers with ReLU, batch normalisation, dropout, Adam, early stopping; softmax output (classes) or linear output (DI). With about 290 training plot-dates per fold the networks are kept very small (e.g. 8–4 units per sensor sub-network, about 1 000 weights), trained with several random seeds, and always compared with the classical models.`],
 mine: `Classification uses SVM, RF, k-NN, DNN-F1, DNN-F2; regression uses SVR, RFR, k-NN, PLSR, DNN-F1, DNN-F2 (${T('models')}).`,
 vis: [TAB('models', [['Model', 'Family', 'Task', 'Key hyperparameters', 'Basis'], ['SVM / SVR', 'Kernel', 'C, R', 'C, γ (ε for SVR)', 'Tian 2021; Mahlein 2019'], ['RF / RFR', 'Tree ensemble', 'C, R', 'trees, mtry, leaf size', 'Feng 2022'], ['k-NN', 'Instance-based', 'C, R', 'k', 'Mustafa 2023'], ['PLSR', 'Linear latent', 'R', 'components', 'Shukla 2024; Guo 2021'], ['DNN-F1', 'Deep, input fusion', 'C, R', 'layers, dropout', 'Maimaitijiang 2020'], ['DNN-F2', 'Deep, intermediate fusion', 'C, R', 'sub-networks, dropout', 'Maimaitijiang 2020']], [1500, 1900, 900, 2500, 2506], 'The six models (C = 4-class classification, R = DI regression).', 16), FIG('dnn', 'f2_dnn_fusion.png', 'DNN-F1 (input-level fusion) and DNN-F2 (intermediate-level fusion), adapted from Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).', 540)],
 out: `Tuned models for each sensor or sensor set, task and fold.`,
 notes: [`**XGBoost** (Chen and Guestrin, 2016).`, `**Gaussian process regression** (Rasmussen and Williams, 2006).`, `**BPNN, ELM, LS-SVM** (Zhu et al., 2017).`],
 qs: [[`What do C and γ do in SVM?`, `C penalises errors (large C fits the training data closely); γ sets how local the RBF kernel is (large γ gives wiggly boundaries).`], [`DNN-F1 vs DNN-F2?`, `DNN-F1 joins all features at the input; DNN-F2 first learns a representation for each sensor and joins them later, which was more accurate and robust in Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).`]],
 refs: `Cortes and Vapnik (1995); Breiman (2001); Cover and Hart (1967); Wold et al. (2001); Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.3. Single sensors versus sensor combinations (fusion)',
 pos: `After Steps 2.1 and 2.2; its results are evaluated in Step 2.4.`,
 concept: `**Feature-level fusion** combines features of several sensors in one model. Two approaches are compared: **ML-SFFS fusion** (DSF pools of the sensors concatenated → VIF → ML-SFFS → model) and **DNN fusion** (DNN-F1, DNN-F2).`,
 need: `Objective 2 asks whether combining sensors improves classification and DI estimation over the best single sensor.`,
 just: `Fusion improved disease estimation in Feng et al. (2022), Liu et al. (2024) and Deng et al. (2024); ML-SFFS fusion of sensors was used by Mustafa et al. (2023); DNN fusion by Maimaitijiang et al. (2020). Mahlein et al. (2019) found that a third sensor did not always help, so each combination is tested.`,
 papers: [`Maimaitijiang et al. (2020): input- and intermediate-level DNN fusion of RGB, MS and thermal.`, `Mustafa et al. (2023): ML-SFFS fusion of features from three sensors.`, `Feng et al. (2022): fusion of spectra, thermal and texture (R² 0.86 in validation); Liu et al. (2024): MS + thermal.`, `Mahlein et al. (2019): HS + thermal best pair; a third sensor did not help.`],
 how: [`Input sets: S1 RGB, S2 MS, S3 HS, S4 TIR; S5 RGB + TIR, S6 MS + TIR, S7 HS + TIR, S8 all four (ORW).`, `For every set and task: ML-SFFS fusion with SVM, RF, k-NN (and PLSR for DI), DNN-F1 and DNN-F2.`, `Fusion is useful only if it beats the best single sensor on the test replications.`],
 mine: `In total 80 model configurations (single sensors: 16 for classification + 20 for regression; combinations: 20 + 24), each tested on three replications.`,
 vis: [FIG('fusionbar', 'm13_fusion.png', 'Dummy comparison of single sensors (grey) and combinations (blue): macro-F1 for classification and R² for DI regression.', 560)],
 out: `Performance of every sensor and combination for both tasks; the sensor make-up of the fused OFCs.`,
 notes: [`**Decision-level fusion** (stacking; Wolpert, 1992).`, `**End-to-end image fusion networks** (Deng et al., 2024).`, `**Attention-based fusion** (Ramachandram and Taylor, 2017).`],
 qs: [[`What if fusion does not help?`, `Then the sensors are redundant for white rust and the cheaper sensor is enough; this is a valid result (Mahlein et al., 2019).`], [`Why test HS + TIR separately?`, `Spectral and thermal sensors measure different processes (pigment vs transpiration); it was the best pair in Mahlein et al. (2019).`]],
 refs: `Mustafa et al. (2023); Maimaitijiang et al. (2020); Feng et al. (2022); Mahlein et al. (2019).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.4. Evaluation and comparison',
 pos: `After Step 2.3 (predictions on held-out replications); it selects the best configuration used in Steps 2.5 and 2.6.`,
 concept: `Classification is evaluated from the confusion matrix: overall accuracy (OA), Cohen's kappa, per-class precision, recall and F1, and macro-F1; regression with R^{2}, RMSE and NRMSE = RMSE/mean DI.`,
 eqs: [EQ('F1_{k} = 2 P_{k} R_{k} / (P_{k} + R_{k});   macro-F1 = (1/4) Σ F1_{k};   RMSE = √[Σ (y − ŷ)^{2} / n]', 'e:metrics')],
 need: `A fair comparison of 80 configurations needs the same metrics on the same test replications.`,
 just: `Macro-F1 weights the four classes equally, which is necessary for unequal class sizes (Sokolova and Lapalme, 2009; Lv et al., 2023); kappa corrects for chance (Cohen, 1960); NRMSE is used because relative error is undefined for DI = 0 (Liu et al., 2024).`,
 papers: [`Liu et al. (2024) and Feng et al. (2022): R² and RMSE for DI.`, `Tian et al. (2021): overall accuracy; Lv et al. (2023): macro-F1 for severity classes.`, `Mahlein et al. (2013): balanced accuracy for indices.`],
 how: [`Metrics per test replication; mean ± SD over three folds.`, `Classes derived from predicted DI are compared with direct classification.`, `Accuracy is also reported separately for each variety (Varuna, Pusa Vijay, PM 38).`, `Overlap check: the share of errors that fall in an adjacent class (± 1 class) is reported (Section 1.2).`],
 mine: `Dummy example: OA ${DUM.cm.oa}, kappa ${DUM.cm.kap}, macro-F1 ${DUM.cm.mf1}; the Moderate class is hardest (F1 ${DUM.cm.F1[2]}); DI regression R^{2} ${DUM.cm.r2}, RMSE ${DUM.cm.rmse}.`,
 vis: [TAB('metrics', __CM__), FIG('eval', 'm14_eval.png', 'Dummy confusion matrix of the 4-class model (a) and observed vs predicted DI (b).', 560)],
 out: `The best sensor set, fusion strategy and model for classification and for regression, with confusion matrices and error statistics.`,
 notes: [`**McNemar's test** to compare classifiers (Dietterich, 1998).`, `**Matthews correlation coefficient** (Chicco and Jurman, 2020).`, `**RPD** for regression (Mandal et al., 2023).`],
 qs: [[`Why macro-F1 and not only accuracy?`, `With unequal classes a model can look accurate while missing the rare High class; macro-F1 gives each class equal weight.`], [`Why NRMSE?`, `Relative error divides by observed DI, which is zero for healthy plots.`]],
 refs: `Cohen (1960); Sokolova and Lapalme (2009); Liu et al. (2024); Maimaitijiang et al. (2020).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.5. Disease maps',
 pos: `After Step 2.4 (best models); before Step 2.6 and as the practical output of Objective 2.`,
 concept: `The best classification and regression models are applied to every plot of a flight to map the class, the DI and the absolute error.`,
 need: `Maps show the spatial pattern of disease and where the models fail.`,
 just: `Plot-level DI and error maps were produced by Liu et al. (2024); class maps from hyperspectral indices by Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).`,
 papers: [`Liu et al. (2024): plot-level DI and error maps.`, `Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): healthy / rot maps from index thresholds.`],
 how: [`Predict each plot of the held-out replication with the best model of each task; map class, DI and |error|.`],
 mine: `One set of maps per flight date for the 72 plots.`,
 vis: [FIG('maps', 'm15_maps.png', 'Dummy maps: observed class, predicted class and absolute DI error.', 560)],
 out: `Class, DI and error maps for every flight.`,
 notes: [`**Uncertainty maps** from class probabilities or quantile regression forests (Meinshausen, 2006).`, `**Kriging** for continuous field maps (Oliver and Webster, 1990).`],
 qs: [[`What do the error maps show?`, `Whether errors are random or concentrated, for example in one variety or near field edges.`]],
 refs: `Liu et al. (2024); Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 2.6. Independent validation',
 pos: `Last step of Objective 2; uses the final models refitted on all IARI data (Step A4).`,
 concept: `The final models are tested on data never used for development: a new site, new varieties and farmers' management.`,
 need: `Cross-validation on one experiment cannot show whether the models transfer to other conditions.`,
 just: `Validation on a separate experiment is the strongest test (Feng et al., 2022); feature sets and models may not transfer between scales or sites (Bohnenkamp et al., 2019).`,
 papers: [`Feng et al. (2022): independent experiment as validation set.`, `Tian et al. (2021) and Mustafa et al. (2023): two seasons.`, `Bohnenkamp et al. (2019): selected features did not transfer between scales.`],
 how: [`Stage 1: IIRMR Bharatpur experiment; Stage 2: adjoining farmers' fields; same ground-truth protocol.`, `Report the same metrics and the loss relative to cross-validation.`],
 mine: `${T('indep')} shows the reporting format with dummy values.`,
 vis: [TAB('indep', [['Dataset', 'macro-F1 (4 classes)', 'R² (DI)', 'RMSE (DI %)'], ['IARI LORO (mean ± SD)', '0.81 ± 0.04', '0.83 ± 0.03', '7.1 ± 0.6'], ['Bharatpur experiment', '0.74', '0.76', '8.9'], ['Farmers\' fields', '0.68', '0.70', '10.2']], [3000, 2100, 2100, 2106], 'Dummy reporting format for independent validation.', 17)],
 out: `Accuracy at Bharatpur and in farmers' fields and the size of the transfer loss.`,
 notes: [`**Domain adaptation** to a new site (Tuia et al., 2016).`, `**Multi-season validation** (Tian et al., 2021; Mustafa et al., 2023).`],
 qs: [[`What if accuracy drops at Bharatpur?`, `It is expected; I report the loss and, if needed, recalibrate with a few local plots.`]],
 refs: `Feng et al. (2022); Bohnenkamp et al. (2019).` });
K.push(BOX('How Objective 2 is satisfied:', `4-class classification and DI regression are modelled with each sensor and with four combinations, using ML-SFFS fusion and DNN fusion; Step 2.4 identifies the best sensor set, fusion and model, Step 2.5 maps the disease and Step 2.6 tests transferability.`, 'FCE4EC', 'AD1457'));

// ======================= 5 OBJECTIVE 3 =======================
K.push(H1('5. Objective 3: Pre-symptomatic prediction'));
FSTEP({ title: 'Step 3.1. Pre-symptomatic samples and labels',
 pos: `Uses the flights before visible symptoms (Step A3) and the later disease scores (Step A2); before Step 3.2.`,
 concept: `Before symptoms DI = 0 everywhere, so the label comes from the future: a control plot is healthy and an inoculated plot is "infected" only if symptoms later appear on it.`,
 need: `Wrong labels are the main risk in pre-symptomatic work.`,
 just: `Retrospective labelling was used for asymptomatic rice blast by Tian et al. (2021).`,
 papers: [`Tian et al. (2021): inoculated sample labelled infected only if symptoms later appeared.`, `Zhu et al. (2017) and Terentev et al. (2023): days after inoculation in controlled experiments.`],
 how: [`Pool all pre-symptomatic dates of the 8 flights (72 plots × number of pre-symptomatic dates; e.g. 2 dates = 144 plot-dates).`, `Inoculated plots that never show symptoms (e.g. PM 38) are excluded from the infected class and reported separately.`],
 mine: `Per pre-symptomatic date: 36 healthy and up to 24 infected plots; with 2 such dates, 72 healthy and up to 48 infected plot-dates (${T('presym')}). The sample is small, so only simple models with few features are used.`,
 vis: [TAB('presym', [['Group', 'Plots', 'Plot-dates (example: 2 pre-symptomatic dates)', 'Label'], ['Controls (D1)', '36', '72', 'Healthy'], ['Inoculated, symptoms later (V1, V2)', 'up to 24', 'up to 48', 'Infected'], ['Inoculated, no symptoms (e.g. PM 38)', 'about 12', 'about 24', 'Excluded, reported separately']], [3500, 1300, 2300, 2206], 'Expected pre-symptomatic class sizes (8 flight dates in total; the number of pre-symptomatic dates is fixed by when symptoms appeared).', 17)],
 out: `A labelled pre-symptomatic table with class counts.`,
 notes: [`**Days after inoculation** under controlled conditions (Zhu et al., 2017; Terentev et al., 2023).`, `**Pathogen quantification** before symptoms (Zhang et al., 2024).`],
 qs: [[`How can you label a plot that looks healthy?`, `By what happens later: it is infected only if symptoms appear on that plot (Tian et al., 2021).`]],
 refs: `Tian et al. (2021); Zhu et al. (2017).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 3.2. Pre-symptomatic features',
 pos: `After Step 3.1 (labels) and using the feature tables of Step 1.1; before Step 3.3.`,
 concept: `Features that already differ between healthy and later-infected plots are found with a **t-test** and the **percentage difference** of the means; **Boruta** then selects the pre-symptomatic DSF pool of each sensor.`,
 need: `Early changes are small and may involve different features (e.g. temperature, PRI) than visible disease.`,
 just: `Two-group screening was used for asymptomatic rice blast (Tian et al., 2021) and Boruta for asymptomatic Fusarium head blight (Mustafa et al., 2023). Thermal and water-related features can change before symptoms (Mahlein et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2024).`,
 papers: [`Mustafa et al. (2023): Boruta for asymptomatic Fusarium head blight.`, `Mahlein et al. (2019): pigment indices (PSSRa/b) separated infected spikelets 3 days after inoculation.`, `Zhang et al. (2024): water (1400 nm) and 750 nm bands drove pre-symptomatic detection.`],
 how: [`t-test and % difference from healthy for every feature (a DI scalogram is not possible because DI = 0); Boruta per sensor → pre-symptomatic DSF pools.`],
 mine: `The dummy example (${F('presymf')}a) ranks thermal features highest, as expected if transpiration changes first.`,
 vis: [FIG('presymf', 'm16_presym.png', 'Dummy pre-symptomatic differences from healthy plots (a) and ROC curves of pre-symptomatic models (b).', 560)],
 out: `Pre-symptomatic DSF pools for each sensor.`,
 notes: [`**Chlorophyll-fluorescence parameters** (Mahlein et al., 2019).`, `**Change relative to control plots** on each flight (Nguyen et al., 2023).`],
 qs: [[`Which sensor do you expect to work earliest?`, `Thermal and hyperspectral, because transpiration and pigments can change before pustules appear.`]],
 refs: `Tian et al. (2021); Mustafa et al. (2023); Mahlein et al. (2019).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 3.3. Pre-symptomatic classification',
 pos: `After Step 3.2; its best model produces the risk maps of Step 3.4.`,
 concept: `Healthy vs later-infected plots are classified with each sensor and each combination, using the same VIF → ML-SFFS chain and SVM, RF and k-NN.`,
 need: `Objective 3 asks whether white rust can be predicted before symptoms and with which sensors.`,
 just: `ML-SFFS with SVM, RF and k-NN detected asymptomatic rice blast and wheat head blight (Tian et al., 2021; Mustafa et al., 2023). DNNs are not used because the pre-symptomatic set (e.g. 144 plot-dates from 72 plots) is far too small.`,
 papers: [`Tian et al. (2021): SVM-SFFS > 65% for asymptomatic rice blast.`, `Mustafa et al. (2023): 87.04% for asymptomatic Fusarium head blight; Lv et al. (2023): SVM 87.1% asymptomatic.`, `Zhu et al. (2017): BPNN, ELM, LS-SVM up to 95% at 48 h; Terentev et al. (2023): SVM 97–100% from 4 days.`],
 how: [`S1–S8; LORO validation; class weights; metrics: sensitivity, specificity, OA, kappa, F1, ROC-AUC (Fawcett, 2006).`],
 mine: `In the dummy example (${F('presymf')}b) HS + TIR gives the highest AUC.`,
 vis: [],
 out: `The best early-warning sensor set and model with sensitivity and specificity.`,
 notes: [`**Anomaly detection** trained on healthy plots only (Liu et al., 2008).`, `**LSTM** on time series of features (Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997).`],
 qs: [[`Why report sensitivity and specificity?`, `Sensitivity is how many infected plots are found; specificity is how many healthy plots are correctly left alone; both matter for spraying decisions.`]],
 refs: `Tian et al. (2021); Mustafa et al. (2023); Fawcett (2006).` });

FSTEP({ title: 'Step 3.4. Risk maps',
 pos: `After Step 3.3; final output of Objective 3.`,
 concept: `The predicted probability of infection of each plot is mapped for each pre-symptomatic flight.`,
 need: `An early-warning system needs a spatial product.`,
 just: `Plot-level mapping follows Liu et al. (2024); combining spectral data with weather-based risk follows the multi-stage scheme of Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).`,
 papers: [`Liu et al. (2024): plot-level maps of model output.`, `Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013): weather-based outbreak conditions combined with spectral discrimination.`],
 how: [`Class probabilities of the best model mapped plot by plot.`],
 mine: `One risk map per pre-symptomatic flight (${F('risk')}).`,
 vis: [FIG('risk', 'm17_risk.png', 'Dummy pre-symptomatic risk map.', 420)],
 out: `Plot-wise infection-probability maps.`,
 notes: [`**Weather-based risk models** (Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay, 2013).`, `**Kriging** of probabilities (Oliver and Webster, 1990).`],
 qs: [[`How would a farmer use the risk map?`, `To inspect and spray high-risk plots first, before the disease is visible.`]],
 refs: `Liu et al. (2024); Bhattacharya and Chattopadhyay (2013).` });
K.push(BOX('How Objective 3 is satisfied:', `The pre-symptomatic models show whether, and with which sensors, white rust can be predicted before symptoms; the risk maps show where infection is developing.`, 'F3E5F5', '6A1B9A'));

// ======================= 6 SUMMARY =======================
